# Real Estate Website Template — environment variable template
#
# This file is committed to the repository as a documentation artifact.
# It is the canonical list of every environment variable the application
# reads. No real credentials, domains, or client names belong here.
#
# Naming convention:
# - NUXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL                  build-time + runtime
# - NUXT_PROPERTIES_* / NUXT_AGENTS_* /
#   NUXT_DEVELOPMENTS_*                   server-only request-time (process.env)
# - NUXT_LEADS_*                          runtime (runtimeConfig, loaded at boot)
# - NUXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL__<TENANT_ID>     per-tenant request-time (process.env)
# - NUXT_LEADS_<KEY>__<TENANT_ID>         per-tenant request-time (process.env)
#
# Lifecycle and purpose:
# - See docs/DEPLOYMENT.md §4 for the build-time vs runtime vs request-time
#   lifecycle of each class.
# - See docs/REBRANDING.md §9 for the production site URL.
# - See docs/REBRANDING.md §12 for the lead-capture pipeline.
# - See docs/MULTI_TENANT.md §2 and §9 for the per-tenant overrides.
#
# Reading this file — every value falls into one of three classes:
#
# 1. REAL DEFAULT — the value shown IS the application's real default.
#    The operator may override it at deploy time; the value displayed
#    is what the application uses when the env var is unset.
#    Examples in this file: `NUXT_PROPERTIES_DATA_SOURCE=static`,
#    `NUXT_PROPERTIES_API_TIMEOUT_MS=10000`, `NUXT_LEADS_ADAPTER=disabled`,
#    `NUXT_LEADS_SMTP_SECURE=` (empty = plaintext SMTP).
#
# 2. REQUIRED DEPLOYMENT PLACEHOLDER — the value shown is NOT the
#    real default (the real default is empty). The operator MUST
#    supply a non-empty value at deploy time. The placeholder is the
#    canonical shape the operator uses as a starting point.
#    Example in this file: `NUXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://example.test`
#    — the real default is empty and the sitemap / robots / canonical
#    metadata are broken without it; replace with the agency's real
#    hostname at deploy time.
#
# 3. OPTIONAL COMMENTED-OUT EXAMPLE OVERRIDE — the line is commented
#    out (begins with `#`) and shows an example value the operator
#    can uncomment and edit. The real default is empty; the variable
#    is required only when the matching `NUXT_*_DATA_SOURCE` is `api`
#    or `cms`.
#    Examples in this file: `NUXT_PROPERTIES_API_URL`,
#    `NUXT_PROPERTIES_CMS_URL`, the per-tenant overrides.
#
# SECRET FIELDS (the webhook signing key and the SMTP password) are
# blank with a comment that names the field. The operator fills the
# real values in the production environment through the platform's
# secret manager — NEVER commit real credentials to the repository.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Site URL (required for SEO infrastructure)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [REQUIRED DEPLOYMENT PLACEHOLDER]
# The real default is empty. The operator MUST supply a non-empty
# value at deploy time — the sitemap returns 503, robots.txt blocks
# all crawling, and <link rel="canonical"> / og:url are omitted
# when this is empty. Trailing slash is stripped automatically.
# Read at build time in nuxt.config.ts (sets the default for
# runtimeConfig.public.siteUrl) and at runtime via Nuxt's automatic
# env-var binding. Replace `https://example.test` with the agency's
# real hostname at deploy time. The value is not a real default
# the application would use when the env var is unset — the
# application is broken without it.
NUXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://example.test

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Data source — properties (optional; default is `static`)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default: bundled sample data under app/features/properties/data/properties.ts.
# Set to `api` to load from a remote HTTP endpoint, or `cms` to load from
# a generic HTTP/JSON CMS driver. The server-only loader
# (server/utils/properties.ts) reads these from process.env on every
# request; no restart required to pick up a change.
NUXT_PROPERTIES_DATA_SOURCE=static

# [OPTIONAL COMMENTED-OUT EXAMPLE OVERRIDE]
# Required when NUXT_PROPERTIES_DATA_SOURCE=api. Real default: empty.
# The loader raises DataSourceMissingConfigError if `api` is selected
# without a URL. Uncomment and replace with the agency's real endpoint.
# NUXT_PROPERTIES_API_URL=https://api.example.test/properties

# [REAL DEFAULT]
# Real default: 10 000 ms (10 seconds). The loader accepts a positive
# integer; any non-integer or non-positive value falls back to the
# 10 000 ms default. The agency may override this at deploy time.
NUXT_PROPERTIES_API_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# [OPTIONAL COMMENTED-OUT EXAMPLE OVERRIDE]
# Required when NUXT_PROPERTIES_DATA_SOURCE=cms. Real default: empty.
# Uncomment and replace with the agency's real CMS endpoint.
# NUXT_PROPERTIES_CMS_URL=https://cms.example.test/properties

# [REAL DEFAULT]
# Real default: 10 000 ms (10 seconds).
NUXT_PROPERTIES_CMS_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Data source — agents (optional; default is `static`)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [REAL DEFAULT] `static`
NUXT_AGENTS_DATA_SOURCE=static
# [OPTIONAL COMMENTED-OUT EXAMPLE OVERRIDE]
# NUXT_AGENTS_API_URL=https://api.example.test/agents
# [REAL DEFAULT] 10 000 ms
NUXT_AGENTS_API_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# [OPTIONAL COMMENTED-OUT EXAMPLE OVERRIDE]
# NUXT_AGENTS_CMS_URL=https://cms.example.test/agents
# [REAL DEFAULT] 10 000 ms
NUXT_AGENTS_CMS_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Data source — developments (optional; default is `static`)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [REAL DEFAULT] `static`
NUXT_DEVELOPMENTS_DATA_SOURCE=static
# [OPTIONAL COMMENTED-OUT EXAMPLE OVERRIDE]
# NUXT_DEVELOPMENTS_API_URL=https://api.example.test/developments
# [REAL DEFAULT] 10 000 ms
NUXT_DEVELOPMENTS_API_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# [OPTIONAL COMMENTED-OUT EXAMPLE OVERRIDE]
# NUXT_DEVELOPMENTS_CMS_URL=https://cms.example.test/developments
# [REAL DEFAULT] 10 000 ms
NUXT_DEVELOPMENTS_CMS_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lead delivery (optional; default is `disabled`)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read at boot via Nuxt's automatic env-var binding to the private
# runtimeConfig block (NUXT_LEADS_ADAPTER -> runtimeConfig.leadsAdapter,
# etc.). The endpoint POST /api/contact reads these per request via
# useRuntimeConfig(). A change requires a server restart.
#
# The visible form is gated by agency.leads.enabled in the agency config
# (default false in the sample agency). The agency must flip BOTH
# flags together to go live:
#   1. app/config/agencies/<agency>.agency.ts -> leads.enabled: true
#   2. NUXT_LEADS_ADAPTER (and adapter-specific env vars) at deploy time
#
# Supported adapters: disabled | log | webhook | email
# - disabled: returns 503 on every submission (the placeholder form is shown).
# - log:      one console.info line per accepted lead (development only).
# - webhook:  POSTs the stamped lead to NUXT_LEADS_WEBHOOK_URL with HMAC
#             SHA-256 signature in the X-Lead-Signature header.
# - email:    sends a plain-text + HTML email through any configured SMTP
#             server via Nodemailer.
# [REAL DEFAULT] `disabled`
NUXT_LEADS_ADAPTER=disabled

# Webhook adapter (required when NUXT_LEADS_ADAPTER=webhook)
# [SECRET FIELD] The real default is empty. The operator fills the
# production value through the platform's secret manager.
NUXT_LEADS_WEBHOOK_URL=
# [SECRET FIELD] 32+ character random string. The real default is empty.
NUXT_LEADS_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Email adapter (required when NUXT_LEADS_ADAPTER=email)
# All values are read from runtimeConfig at boot. Empty values are
# rejected by the adapter's required-config validation — the endpoint
# surfaces a 502 with a clear "unsupported" errorCode and no SMTP
# connection is attempted.
NUXT_LEADS_SMTP_HOST=
# [REAL DEFAULT] empty. The adapter requires a positive integer;
# ports like 587 (plaintext / STARTTLS) and 465 (implicit TLS) are
# the most common values. Strings are rejected.
NUXT_LEADS_SMTP_PORT=
# [REAL DEFAULT] empty (= plaintext SMTP). The adapter accepts the
# string "true" (case-insensitive) to force TLS; any other value
# (including empty, "false", "1") means plaintext SMTP. The
# STARTTLS upgrade (port 587 + NUXT_LEADS_SMTP_SECURE unset) is the
# most common production setup.
NUXT_LEADS_SMTP_SECURE=
NUXT_LEADS_SMTP_USER=
# [SECRET FIELD]
NUXT_LEADS_SMTP_PASSWORD=
NUXT_LEADS_EMAIL_FROM=
NUXT_LEADS_EMAIL_TO=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-tenant overrides — multi-tenant deployments only
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The tenant id is uppercased and any non-alphanumeric character is
# replaced with `_` for the env-var name. The per-tenant override
# takes precedence over the global; the global is the fallback.
# Per-tenant overrides are read by the per-request resolver from
# process.env directly (Nuxt's runtimeConfig is global, so per-tenant
# overrides bypass it).
#
# [OPTIONAL COMMENTED-OUT EXAMPLE OVERRIDES]
# Real default: empty. Most deployments do not need per-tenant
# overrides; the global env var is the default. Uncomment and edit
# only when the deployment hosts multiple agencies behind a single
# build and one tenant needs a different value.
# NUXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL__DEFAULT=https://default.example.test
# NUXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL__ACME=https://acme.example.test
# NUXT_LEADS_ADAPTER__ACME=webhook
# NUXT_LEADS_WEBHOOK_URL__ACME=https://hooks.example.test/acme
# NUXT_LEADS_WEBHOOK_SECRET__ACME=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sanity CMS (Task 115 — v1.2 pilot) — optional
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Sanity driver is selected when `NUXT_<FEATURE>_DATA_SOURCE=cms`
# AND `NUXT_<FEATURE>_CMS_PROVIDER=sanity` for the property / agent /
# development features. The configuration is shared across the
# three features (one Sanity project, one dataset, one token);
# the GROQ query and per-record mapping are the only feature
# pieces. See `docs/CMS_EVALUATION.md` §6.1 for the full design.
#
# [REAL DEFAULT] empty when the provider is not `sanity`.
# The Sanity driver is constructed server-only (`server/utils/
# sanity-config.ts`) and the read token never reaches the client
# bundle. The token is stored in the platform's secret manager.
NUXT_SANITY_PROJECT_ID=
# [REAL DEFAULT] `production` (applied when unset).
NUXT_SANITY_DATASET=production
# [REAL DEFAULT] `2024-01-01` (applied when unset). Pinned at
# build time so the GROQ query result shape is stable.
NUXT_SANITY_API_VERSION=2024-01-01
# [REAL DEFAULT] empty. Required for a private dataset; for a
# public dataset the token is empty.
NUXT_SANITY_TOKEN=
# [REAL DEFAULT] `http-json` (applied when unset). Set to
# `sanity` to select the Sanity driver for that feature. The
# cms branch in `server-data-source.ts` omits `endpointEnvName`
# when the provider is Sanity, so `NUXT_<FEATURE>_CMS_URL` is
# NOT required for the Sanity path.
NUXT_PROPERTIES_CMS_PROVIDER=http-json
NUXT_AGENTS_CMS_PROVIDER=http-json
NUXT_DEVELOPMENTS_CMS_PROVIDER=http-json

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sanity webhook integration (Task 128 — v1.3) — optional
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Sanity Studio webhook receiver ships in `server/api/webhooks/
# sanity.post.ts`. It is a Node/Nitro-only endpoint (a static
# `pnpm generate` deployment has no Nitro runtime to host it;
# see `docs/SANITY_OPERATIONS.md` for the static deployment's
# separate flow). The endpoint verifies the Sanity signature
# against the raw request body, deduplicates on the
# `idempotency-key` header (Sanity uses at-least-once delivery),
# and POSTs to the externally-configured deploy hook below.
#
# The endpoint NEVER follows an arbitrary redirect with the
# deployment credentials attached (the deploy hook uses
# `redirect: 'manual'` and a bounded 5-second AbortController
# timeout, see `server/utils/deploy-trigger.ts`). The endpoint
# NEVER logs the raw body, the secret, or the provider response
# body — only structured fields (webhook id, operation,
# document id, idempotency key, dispatch outcome, status).
#
# The secret is read server-only via `process.env` (NEVER via
# `useRuntimeConfig`); it never enters the public runtime config
# the client bundle can read. Multi-tenant deployments override
# per-tenant via `NUXT_SANITY_WEBHOOK_SECRET__<TENANT_ID>` (the
# tenant id is uppercased + non-alphanumeric replaced with `_`,
# matching the `NUXT_LEADS_*__<TENANT_ID>` convention).
#
# [REAL DEFAULT] empty when the Sanity webhook integration is
# not used. The endpoint returns 401 on a missing / malformed /
# stale / mismatched signature; a missing secret returns 401
# with the same shape so the operator's log surfaces the
# misconfiguration without leaking signature data.
NUXT_SANITY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# [REAL DEFAULT] empty. The same value can be overridden per
# tenant via the convention `NUXT_SANITY_WEBHOOK_SECRET__<TENANT_ID>`.
# Uncomment and edit only when the deployment hosts multiple
# agencies behind a single build and one tenant needs its own
# secret.
# NUXT_SANITY_WEBHOOK_SECRET__ACME=
# [REAL DEFAULT] `300000` (5 minutes, applied when unset).
# The signature verifier's replay-protection tolerance. The
# tolerance is a PROJECT replay-protection policy; it is NOT
# a requirement of Sanity's signature protocol (Sanity's
# reference `@sanity/webhook` library does not enforce a
# tolerance window at all). The 5-minute default matches the
# Stripe convention the Sanity docs reference and the
# realistic clock-skew envelope between Sanity's delivery
# infrastructure and a Nitro endpoint behind a CDN. A larger
# window is safe for an unusual deployment; a smaller window
# tightens the replay window at the cost of false-positive
# rejections.
NUXT_SANITY_WEBHOOK_TOLERANCE_MS=300000
# [REAL DEFAULT] `3600000` (1 hour, applied when unset).
# The dedup cache's TTL per cached entry. Separate from the
# signature verifier's tolerance: the verifier's tolerance is
# a SECURITY boundary (rejects replay attempts older than the
# window); the dedup TTL is an OPERATIONAL courtesy (recognizes
# a replayed delivery as "already dispatched" without
# re-invoking the trigger). The two env vars are deliberately
# independent so an operator can tighten one without relaxing
# the other. The 1-hour default is conservative: Sanity's
# documented retry window is seconds-to-minutes, so any
# replay that lands more than 1 hour after the original
# success is treated as a fresh delivery. The deploy hook
# must be idempotent on its own end to make this safe.
NUXT_SANITY_WEBHOOK_DEDUP_TTL_MS=3600000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# External rebuild trigger (Task 128 — v1.3) — optional
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The provider-agnostic deploy hook URL the Sanity webhook
# endpoint POSTs to after a verified Sanity content change. The
# endpoint NEVER names a specific commercial hosting provider;
# the operator chooses where the URL points. Typical receivers:
#
#  - The hosting platform's deploy hook (the operator's hosting
#    platform exposes one). This is the recommended shape for
#    static deployments (the webhook triggers the platform's
#    rebuild pipeline directly, no separate receiver needed).
#  - A CI workflow with a `repository_dispatch` trigger fired
#    from a small serverless receiver (Cloudflare Worker,
#    Lambda, edge function, Vercel edge middleware). The
#    receiver translates the Sanity webhook into a
#    `repository_dispatch` event with `event_type:
#    'sanity-webhook'`.
#  - A GitHub Actions `workflow_dispatch` triggered via the
#    GitHub REST API from a similar receiver.
#
# The endpoint treats the receiver's 2xx as success. Under the
# failure-safe retry semantics, every non-2xx dispatch outcome
# (auth rejected, not found, client error, server error, timeout,
# network error, redirect blocked) returns HTTP 503
# (retryable — Sanity retries; the endpoint releases the
# idempotency-key claim so the retry can re-claim and
# re-dispatch). `missing_config` also returns 503 (Sanity
# retries so the operator sees the retry traffic and configures
# the hook).
#
# The endpoint NEVER follows an arbitrary redirect with the
# deployment credentials attached. The deploy hook uses
# `redirect: 'manual'` and a bounded AbortController timeout so
# a slow or redirecting receiver becomes a 503 / Sanity retry
# rather than a hung endpoint.
#
# [REQUIRED FOR SANITY WEBHOOK RECEIVER WHEN NODE/NITRO]
# Empty by default — the Sanity webhook integration is
# opt-in. The endpoint returns 503 (`missing_dispatch_config`)
# when this var is empty, so Sanity retries until the operator
# configures it.
NUXT_DEPLOY_HOOK_URL=
# [OPTIONAL]
# Empty by default. When set, the trigger forwards the value as
# the `Authorization` header on the deploy-hook POST. Common
# shapes: `Bearer <token>` (Vercel, Netlify), `Token <token>`
# (GitHub PAT for `repository_dispatch`). The trigger never
# logs the value — it appears only in the outbound HTTP
# request, never in the endpoint's structured log lines.
NUXT_DEPLOY_HOOK_AUTH_HEADER=
# [REAL DEFAULT] `5000` (5 seconds, applied when unset). The
# outer timeout for the deploy-hook POST. The Sanity delivery
# timeout is 30 seconds; a 5-second receiver timeout leaves
# headroom for the signature verification + dispatch path.
# Increase for slow receivers; decrease for tighter SLAs.
NUXT_DEPLOY_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sanity Studio (Task 118 — v1.2 pilot) — separate project under `studio/`
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Sanity Studio is the source of truth for the agency-owned
# Property / Agent / Development content model. The Studio is a
# separate npm project (its own package.json + pnpm-lock.yaml
# under `studio/`) and does NOT share the Nuxt app's dependencies.
# The Studio is deployed to Sanity's managed hosting with
# `cd studio && pnpm deploy`. See `studio/README.md` for the full
# setup, deploy, and access workflow.
#
# The Studio reads its project ID + dataset from the environment
# when it runs locally (`studio/.env`) and when it is deployed
# (the `sanity deploy` command reads the same env vars). The
# values must match the Sanity project the agency owns. The
# Studio's `sanity.config.ts` and `sanity.cli.ts` both read these
# vars.
#
# IMPORTANT: every `SANITY_STUDIO_*` env var is inlined into the
# Studio's JavaScript bundle at build time (Sanity is a Vite-built
# SPA, publicly served at <projectId>.sanity.studio). NEVER place
# a token, a key, or any other secret in a `SANITY_STUDIO_*` env
# var. The Studio's only configuration is the project ID + dataset
# name; secrets live in the Nuxt app's runtime config
# (`NUXT_SANITY_TOKEN`, read server-only from `process.env` inside
# `server/utils/sanity-config.ts`). The Studio never sees the read
# token.
#
# [REQUIRED DEPLOYMENT PLACEHOLDER]
# `SANITY_STUDIO_PROJECT_ID` has no real default. The Studio
# refuses to boot without it (`studio/sanity.config.ts` throws a
# clear error at startup). The agency creates the project at
# https://www.sanity.io/manage and copies the project ID. The
# value is the same string the Nuxt app reads via
# `NUXT_SANITY_PROJECT_ID`.
# SANITY_STUDIO_PROJECT_ID=
# [REAL DEFAULT]
# `production` (applied when unset). Override only when the
# project uses a non-default dataset (`staging`, `development`,
# etc.). The Studio refuses to publish when the dataset does not
# exist — the operator must verify the dataset via the Sanity
# project management UI or `pnpm exec sanity dataset create`
# before the first publish (see `studio/README.md` §5).
# SANITY_STUDIO_DATASET=production
